
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that's the option I'd have recommended. It gives us a seam to design against instead of a client-side design
  we'd have to unpick later.

  [1mNext fork: what "a record of who logged in" means.[0m These sound alike and produce different data models, different
  storage, and different cutover work when the server arrives.

  - [1mCurrent identity only[0m — one stored value answering "who is logged in right now," read on page load so other forms
    know the user without re-asking. Fixed size, trivially swappable for a server session later. Answers "who is here,"
    not "who logged in on Tuesday."
  - [1mAn event log[0m — append a record per login attempt (id, username, timestamp, success/failure). This is the
    audit-shaped reading of your words. In [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m it grows without bound and needs an explicit cap and eviction
    rule, and it's still forgeable until the server owns it — so treat it as a staging area that ships to the backend on
    cutover, not as the real log.
  - [1mBoth[0m — current identity for the app to read, plus a bounded recent-event buffer.

  The distinction that matters for cost: current identity is a value other code [3mdepends on[0m, so its shape is expensive to
  change once other forms read it. The event log is write-mostly and nothing branches on it, so it stays cheap to
  change.

  I recommend [1mboth[0m, with the log capped and explicitly labeled provisional. "Other forms will need it later" needs the
  current identity; "we have a record of who logged in" needs the log; and building only one of them means revisiting
  this.
